  "id": "COMPOUND:21671 - PROTEIN:4886",
  "verbalization": "Massarinin B is shown to have a target relationship with Serine/threonine-protein kinase ATR through three distinct pathways, all of which involve the enzyme ATR as a downstream target. Pathway 1 links Massarinin B to SK-HEP1, which is known to be a target of a complex compound, and ultimately to ATR. Pathway 2 connects Massarinin B to the Glucagon receptor, which is targeted by another compound, and again leads to ATR. Pathway 3 shows Massarinin B targeting Aconitate hydratase, a mitochondrial enzyme, which is targeted by a complex compound and also leads to ATR. These pathways collectively suggest that Massarinin B may modulate ATR through different molecular mechanisms, possibly involving enzyme inhibition or receptor signaling. The evidence supports a potential role for Massarinin B in regulating ATR activity, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The consistent involvement of ATR as a downstream target across these pathways implies a plausible biological role for Massarinin B in influencing ATR function, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the specific mechanism.",
